@@ -24,7 +24,7 @@ The desktop project references the original `SnowPakTool` project and calls its
 
 The **Game data → Trucks-Cargo** sub-tab lists cargo types and carrier variants from the active workspace, including DLC and sparse workspace updates. Search by cargo name, type, variant, or content pack; click column headers to sort. The table shows the defined cargo length, sum of addon physics body masses, and cargo value. Log cargo length represents carrier slots; body mass can include carrier parts. Blank numeric values mean the value is not explicitly defined. Select a row for its localized description and all attributes from the cargo type and addon source files. **Reload cargo** refreshes the workspace data after external edits. This catalog is read-only.
 
-The **Game data → Regions-Trucks** sub-tab reads truck placements from `[scene]/data_for_ssl.bin` inside installed `level_*.pak` files, or from extracted `level_*/[scene]/data_for_ssl.bin` folders. It detects common Steam install locations; **Map folder** can be changed manually. Select one or more region tiles in the top row to populate the table below. Selected tiles are highlighted. The **Obtainable** and **Mission** checkboxes independently control those truck groups; unverified rows always remain visible for review. Double-tap **Shift** to open the same search overlay as Tier List: three or more characters highlight matches and scroll to the first; **Enter** selects a match and **Esc** closes the overlay. Search does not hide rows. Each placement remains a separate row, so collectible and mission-only copies of the same model are distinguishable.
+The **Game data → Regions-Trucks** sub-tab reads truck placements from `[scene]/data_for_ssl.bin` inside installed `level_*.pak` files, or from extracted `level_*/[scene]/data_for_ssl.bin` folders. It detects common Steam install locations; **Map folder** can be changed manually. Select one or more region tiles in the top row to populate the table below. Selected tiles are highlighted. The **Obtainable** and **Mission** checkboxes independently control those truck groups; unverified rows always remain visible for review. Double-tap **Shift** to open the same search overlay as Tier List: any nonempty text highlights matches and scrolls to the first; **Enter** selects a match. **Esc**, clicking elsewhere, switching windows, or leaving the page closes the overlay. Search covers all visible table values, hover details, evidence, source paths and region tile text; Tier List also searches tier/group names and descriptions. Search does not hide rows. Each placement remains a separate row, so collectible and mission-only copies of the same model are distinguishable.
 
 Region tiles retain their 150 × 210 size and are sorted by original release order: the base-game regions followed by seasons 1–19. Each tile has a region-name header, a representative map screenshot from [MapRunner](https://www.maprunner.info/), and a season/counts footer. Thumbnails are embedded for offline use; the source manifest is in `Assets/Regions/sources.json`. Unrecognized future region IDs sort after known releases and retain a text fallback.
 
@@ -34,6 +34,8 @@ Confirmed placement exceptions take precedence over inferred task rules. The 17
 
 The truck table's **Tires** and **Addons** columns show each placement's map equipment, resolving blank configuration fields from the active workspace's vehicle defaults. Tires display **Highway**, **All-terrain**, **Offroad**, **Mudtires**, **Ice** (chains), or **Custom** (a tire definition compatible with only that truck in the current workspace). Hover over Tires for the specific tire name, wheel family, tire ID, rim and default/override information. Parent wheel definitions are followed when checking compatibility. If a map references a missing tire variant, the category is inferred from its stored ID where possible and the tooltip identifies the unresolved variant; unreadable equipment remains **Unknown**. Addons include explicitly installed equipment and default addons, with a map addon replacing the stock addon in the same socket. Hover over Addons for full names, IDs and default/map origins. These describe map placements rather than changes in a player save.
 
+In this application, **visual addons** specifically means addon definitions stored in a folder whose name ends in `_tuning`. This user-defined grouping applies even to functional parts (such as diff locks or snorkels) placed in those folders. Each installed addon retains its source path, original game `Category`, map/default origin, and `IsVisual` flag in the catalog; the addon tooltip shows the grouping and category. Future requests to hide visual addons should use this folder-based flag rather than guessing from part names or gameplay effects.
+
 The catalog matches placement IDs to nested task/contract settings in the active workspace. Explicit `REWARD` actions indicate obtainable task vehicles; `DESTROY` and `mustDestroyAfterSubstage` indicate mission-only vehicles. Untagged world placements are labeled **Obtainable — discovery/start**, an inference that includes starting vehicles. Named placements without an explicit ownership rule, and conflicting reward/removal rules, remain **Unverified**. Select a row for the classification evidence, map coordinates, and source files. This is a catalog of the selected map files, not the player's save progress. Use a workspace from the same game version as the maps. Reading does not modify or extract the source archives.
 
 Packing keeps `[strings]` as top-level PAK content; only `[ps]` and `[ps_common]` are written into `initial.cache_block`. Regular assets use ZIP deflate compression (while `pak.load_list` and `.pct_header` files are stored) to avoid producing archives several times larger than the original.
